solution:
Characteristics of the transition elements — all traceable to partly filled \((n-1)d\) orbitals:

- variable oxidation states differing by unity;
- formation of coloured ions and compounds (\(d\)-\(d\) transitions);
- paramagnetism, from unpaired \(d\) electrons;
- a strong tendency to form complexes, because of small size, high charge and vacant \(d\) orbitals;
- catalytic activity, both heterogeneous and homogeneous;
- formation of interstitial compounds and of alloys;
- typical metallic properties: hard, high density, high melting and boiling points, high enthalpy of atomisation, good conductors of heat and electricity.

Why "transition": they occupy the region of the periodic table BETWEEN the \(s\)-block and the \(p\)-block, and their properties are transitional between the highly reactive, ionic, electropositive \(s\)-block metals and the largely covalent \(p\)-block elements. (The name predates the modern IUPAC definition, which is based on the incomplete \(d\) subshell.)

Which \(d\)-block elements are not transition elements: zinc, cadmium, mercury and copernicium of group 12. Their configuration is \((n-1)d^{10}ns^{2}\), so the \(d\) subshell is completely filled in the ground state AND in their common oxidation state \(\text{M}^{2+}\) (\(d^{10}\)). Having no partly filled \(d\) subshell in any stable state, they fail the definition — and correspondingly they are not coloured, not paramagnetic and show no variable oxidation states.

The lanthanoids are "smooth" because essentially every member shows the same characteristic \(+3\) state, so the series varies only gradually and predictably; the \(4f\) orbitals are buried too deeply to take part in bonding, and departures from \(+3\) are confined to a handful of elements (\(\text{Ce}^{4+}\), \(\text{Tb}^{4+}\), \(\text{Sm}^{2+}\), \(\text{Eu}^{2+}\), \(\text{Yb}^{2+}\)) where an empty, half-filled or full \(4f\) subshell results.

The actinoids are not smooth because the \(5f\), \(6d\) and \(7s\) levels lie very close in energy, so a variable number of electrons is available for bonding and each early element has its own set of oxidation states. Examples from the oxidation states:

- Th shows mainly \(+4\), not \(+3\);
- Pa shows \(+3, +4\) and \(+5\);
- U shows \(+3, +4, +5\) and \(+6\), the last being the important one (as \(\text{UO}_2^{2+}\));
- Np and Pu extend as far as \(+7\);
- only from americium onwards does \(+3\) settle down as the dominant state, so behaviour resembling the lanthanoids is not evident until the second half of the series.

Thus the maximum oxidation state RISES from \(+4\) at thorium to \(+7\) around neptunium and plutonium and then FALLS BACK to \(+3\) for the later members — there is no single characteristic state running through the series as there is for the lanthanoids. Add to this that every actinoid is radioactive, several are available only in trace amounts, and the study of their chemistry is correspondingly difficult.

Members showing \(+4\): cerium, praseodymium, neodymium, terbium and dysprosium. Of these only \(\text{Ce}^{4+}\) is important in solution; \(\text{Pr}^{4+}\), \(\text{Nd}^{4+}\) and \(\text{Dy}^{4+}\) are confined to solid oxides and fluorides.

Members showing \(+2\): samarium, europium and ytterbium (and thulium in the solid state).

Correlation with the \(4f\) configuration. The characteristic state is \(+3\); a lanthanoid departs from it when doing so produces an EMPTY, exactly HALF-FILLED or completely FULL \(4f\) subshell, all of which carry extra stability:

- \(\text{Ce}^{4+}\) is \(4f^{0}\) — the empty subshell;
- \(\text{Tb}^{4+}\) is \(4f^{7}\) — half-filled;
- \(\text{Eu}^{2+}\) is \(4f^{7}\) — half-filled;
- \(\text{Yb}^{2+}\) is \(4f^{14}\) — completely filled.

(\(\text{Sm}^{2+}\) at \(4f^{6}\) and \(\text{Pr}^{4+}\) at \(4f^{1}\) are one electron short of, or one beyond, such a configuration, which is why they exist but are much less stable.)

The consequence is that all these ions revert to \(+3\) readily: the \(+4\) ions are oxidising agents (\(\text{Ce}^{4+}\) is a standard laboratory oxidant, \(E^{\circ} \approx +1.74\,\text{V}\)) and the \(+2\) ions are reducing agents (\(\text{Eu}^{2+}\) reduces water).
